m\\\" (if named)\\n   102→- \\\"my father\\\" might refer to \\\"Alphonse Frankenstein\\\"\\n   103→\\n   104→CRITICAL RULES:\\n   105→1. Only link when context CLEARLY indicates they're the same person\\n   106→2. Use chapter co-appearances and context clues\\n   107→3. Titles/roles (the king, the general, my father) can refer to properly-named characters\\n   108→4. Generic epithets that could be anyone should NOT be linked\\n   109→5. DO NOT link if the epithet and proper name are in CONFLICT, OPPOSITION, or CONFRONTATION\\n   110→   - If context shows them interacting as separate entities, they are different people\\n   111→   - Examples: \\\"the intruder\\\" confronting \\\"Prince Prospero\\\", \\\"the stranger\\\" fighting \\\"the hero\\\"\\n   112→\\n   113→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   114→\\n   115→CROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \\\"\\\"\\\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\\n   116→\\n   117→DESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\\n   118→{epithets}\\n   119→\\n   120→NAMED CHARACTERS:\\n   121→{proper_names}\\n   122→\\n   123→IMPORTANT: Read the context snippets carefully. If an epithet and proper name appear in contexts showing them as SEPARATE ENTITIES (confronting, fighting, observing each other), DO NOT link them.\\n   124→\\n   125→For each epithet that CLEARLY refers to a named character, return a match.\\n   126→\\n   127→Return JSON array:\\n   128→```json\\n   129→[\\n   130→  {{\\\"epithet\\\": \\\"the prince\\\", \\\"proper_name\\\": \\\"Prince Prospero\\\"}},\\n   131→  {{\\\"epithet\\\": \\\"my father\\\", \\\"proper_name\\\": \\\"Alphonse Frankenstein\\\"}}\\n   132→]\\n   133→```\\n   134→\\n   135→Return ONLY the JSON array. Include only matches you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   136→\\n   137→PAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \\\"\\\"\\\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether two character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\\n   138→\\n   139→CRITICAL:\\n   140→- Use ONLY the evidence provided below (chapter appearances and context snippets).\\n   141→- Be conservative: false negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different people).\\n   142→- Titles (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Dr/etc.) are not part of the name; treat them as address forms.\\n   143→\\n   144→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   145→\\n   146→PAIRWISE_ALIAS_PROMPT = \\\"\\\"\\\"Decide whether these two names refer to the SAME PERSON in this novel.\\n   147→\\n   148→NAME A: {name_a}\\n   149→MENTIONS A: {mentions_a}\\n   150→CHAPTERS A: {chapters_a}\\n   151→CONTEXT A:\\n   152→{contexts_a}\\n   153→\\n   154→NAME B: {name_b}\\n   155→MENTIONS B: {mentions_b}\\n   156→CHAPTERS B: {chapters_b}\\n   157→CONTEXT B:\\n   158→{contexts_b}\\n   159→\\n   160→Rules:\\n   161→- Only say same_person=true if there is strong evidence they are the same character (name overlap, title variants, obvious nickname/full-name relationship, or clear contextual cues).\\n   162→- If the only overlap is a last name, be VERY cautious (family members/spouses share last names).\\n   163→- If they never appear in overlapping chapters, be cautious unless it's clearly a full-name/short-name relationship.\\n   164→\\n   165→Return JSON:\\n   166→{{\\n   167→  \\\"same_person\\\": true/false,\\n   168→  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.0-1.0,\\n   169→  \\\"canonical\\\": \\\"Which of the two names should be canonical if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\\\",\\n   170→  \\\"alias\\\": \\\"The other name if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\\\",\\n   171→  \\\"reason\\\": \\\"brief justification\\\"\\n   172→}}\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   173→\\n   174→\\n   175→ALIAS_RESOLUTION_SYSTEM = \\\"\\\"\\\"You are a literary analyst identifying character aliases.\\n   176→\\n   177→Your task is to determine which character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\\n   178→\\n   179→IMPORTANT: Titles, ranks, and honorifics should be IGNORED when matching names.\\n   180→The underlying PERSON is what matters, not how they're addressed. Examples:\\n   181→- Military: \\\"SSgt Otto\\\" = \\\"Staff Sergeant Mark Otto\\\" = \\\"Mark Otto\\\" (but NOT just \\\"Otto\\\" alone)\\n   182→- Civilian: \\\"Mr. Smith\\\" = \\\"John Smith\\\" (but NOT just \\\"Smith\\\" alone - could be family member)\\n   183→- Religious: \\\"Father O'Brien\\\" = \\\"Patrick O'Brien\\\" (but NOT just \\\"O'Brien\\\" alone)\\n   184→- Academic: \\\"Professor Williams\\\" = \\\"Dr. Williams\\\" = \\\"Jane Williams\\\"\\n   185→\\n   186→CRITICAL RULES - BE CONSERVATIVE:\\n   187→1. Only merge names if they CLEARLY refer to the same individual with HIGH CONFIDENCE\\n   188→2. Different people with similar names should NEVER be merged\\n   189→3. Pay close attention to chapter appearances and context clues\\n   190→4. When in doubt, keep names SEPARATE - false negatives are better than false positives\\n   191→5. A bare FIRST name can merge with a full name (e.g., \\\"Mike\\\" → \\\"Michael Anderson\\\")\\n   192→6. A bare LAST name is RISKY - only merge if you're absolutely certain (could be family member)\\n   193→\\n   194→For the canonical name, prefer the FULL CIVILIAN NAME (first + last) over titled versions.\\n   195→\\n   196→NEVER merge:\\n   197→- Different characters who happen to share only a first name (e.g., two characters named Michael)\\n   198→- Different characters who share ONLY a last name (family members, spouses, siblings)\\n   199→  - \\\"Mr. Anderson\\\" (father) ≠ \\\"Michael\\\" (son) even if both are Andersons\\n   200→  - \\\"Robert Wilson\\\" ≠ \\\"Mrs. Wilson\\\" (his wife Emma)\\n   201→- Characters who appear in completely different parts of the book with no overlap\\n   202→- Names that are clearly different major characters (check mention counts - if both have 50+ mentions, they're probably separate people!)\\n   203→\\n   204→BE EXTREMELY CAUTIOUS WITH:\\n   205→- Bare last names (could be family members)\\n   206→- Gendered titles (Mr. vs Mrs. vs Miss suggests different people)\\n   207→- Characters with similar but different names (e.g., \\\"Michael\\\" vs \\\"Michelle\\\")\\n   208→\\n   209→SPECIFIC ANTI-PATTERNS (DO NOT MERGE):\\n   210→- Different first names + same last name (likely family members): spouses, siblings, parents/children\\n   211→- Single-word names with no overlap: completely different characters with unrelated names\\n   212→- Characters who co-occur but have different identities: appearing together ≠ being the same person\\n   213→\\n   214→ONLY merge when confident:\\n   215→1. Full name + partial name: full name contains the partial name as a component\\n   216→2. Title variations: titled form and untitled form of the same name\\n   217→3. Obvious nickname patterns: common nickname/full name pairs\\n   218→\\n   219→CRITICAL MERGE PATTERNS:\\n   220→\\n   221→MERGE these patterns (HIGH CONFIDENCE):\\n   222→- Full name variations: \\\"FirstName LastName\\\" = \\\"LastName\\\" = \\\"Title LastName\\\"\\n   223→- Nickname patterns: Shortened forms or diminutives of the same character\\n   224→- Birth/married names: Known alternate names for the same person (when contextually clear)\\n   225→\\n   226→DO NOT MERGE these patterns:\\n   227→- Title-only matches: Characters sharing only a title but different last names are DIFFERENT people\\n   228→- Family members: Different first names + same last name typically indicates spouses, siblings, or family (separate characters)\\n   229→- Similar titles: Same professional title (Professor, Doctor, Captain) doesn't indicate same person\\n   230→\\n   231→CONTEXTUAL CLUES to consider:\\n   232→- Relationship descriptors: \\\"his/her spouse\\\", \\\"his/her parent\\\", \\\"his/her sibling\\\" indicate SEPARATE characters\\n   233→- Gendered pronouns: Consistent he/she usage helps distinguish characters with ambiguous names\\n   234→- First-person narrative: First-person \\\"I\\\" may indicate narrator (check for self-references)\\n   235→\\n   236→MENTION COUNT HEURISTIC:\\n   237→- Large disparity (e.g., 200+ vs <10 mentions) often indicates full name vs shortened form\\n   238→- Similar counts (both >20 mentions) often indicates distinct characters of comparable importance\\n   239→\\n   240→When in doubt, keep SEPARATE. False negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different characters).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   241→\\n   242→\\n   243→ALIAS_RESOLUTION_PROMPT = \\\"\\\"\\\"Identify which character names refer to the SAME PERSON in this novel.\\n   244→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"10a8e652-7846-4dba-89c8-03dc3ba0817f\",\"uuid\":\"e5ff9b10-16e9-440f-aa28-4d68580a5cd1\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py\",\"content\":\"# Cross-group (epithet-to-proper-name) resolution prompt\\nCROSS_GROUP_SYSTEM = \\\"\\\"\\\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether descriptive handles (epithets) refer to named characters.\\n\\nExamples:\\n- \\\"the prince\\\" might refer to \\\"Prince Prospero\\\"\\n- \\\"the duke\\\" might refer to \\\"Prince Prospero\\\" (if the story mentions he's also a duke)\\n- \\\"the creature\\\" might refer to \\\"Adam\\\" (if named)\\n- \\\"my father\\\" might refer to \\\"Alphonse Frankenstein\\\"\\n\\nCRITICAL RULES:\\n1. Only link when context CLEARLY indicates they're the same person\\n2. Use chapter co-appearances and context clues\\n3. Titles/roles (the king, the general, my father) can refer to properly-named characters\\n4. Generic epithets that could be anyone should NOT be linked\\n5. DO NOT link if the epithet and proper name are in CONFLICT, OPPOSITION, or CONFRONTATION\\n   - If context shows them interacting as separate entities, they are different people\\n   - Examples: \\\"the intruder\\\" confronting \\\"Prince Prospero\\\", \\\"the stranger\\\" fighting \\\"the hero\\\"\\n\\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nCROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \\\"\\\"\\\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\\n\\nDESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\\n{epithets}\\n\\nNAMED CHARACTERS:\\n{proper_names}\\n\\nIMPORTANT: Read the context snippets carefully. If an epithet and proper name appear in contexts showing them as SEPARATE ENTITIES (confronting, fighting, observing each other), DO NOT link them.\\n\\nFor each epithet that CLEARLY refers to a named character, return a match.\\n\\nReturn JSON array:\\n```json\\n[\\n  {{\\\"epithet\\\": \\\"the prince\\\", \\\"proper_name\\\": \\\"Prince Prospero\\\"}},\\n  {{\\\"epithet\\\": \\\"my father\\\", \\\"proper_name\\\": \\\"Alphonse Frankenstein\\\"}}\\n]\\n```\\n\\nReturn ONLY the JSON array. Include only matches you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nPAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \\\"\\\"\\\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether two character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\\n\\nCRITICAL:\\n- Use ONLY the evidence provided below (chapter appearances and context snippets).\\n- Be conservative: false negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different people).\\n- Titles (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Dr/etc.) are not part of the name; treat them as address forms.\\n\\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nPAIRWISE_ALIAS_PROMPT = \\\"\\\"\\\"Decide whether these two names refer to the SAME PERSON in this novel.\\n\\nNAME A: {name_a}\\nMENTIONS A: {mentions_a}\\nCHAPTERS A: {chapters_a}\\nCONTEXT A:\\n{contexts_a}\\n\\nNAME B: {name_b}\\nMENTIONS B: {mentions_b}\\nCHAPTERS B: {chapters_b}\\nCONTEXT B:\\n{contexts_b}\\n\\nRules:\\n- Only say same_person=true if there is strong evidence they are the same character (name overlap, title variants, obvious nickname/full-name relationship, or clear contextual cues).\\n- If the only overlap is a last name, be VERY cautious (family members/spouses share last names).\\n- If they never appear in overlapping chapters, be cautious unless it's clearly a full-name/short-name relationship.\\n\\nReturn JSON:\\n{{\\n  \\\"same_person\\\": true/false,\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.0-1.0,\\n  \\\"canonical\\\": \\\"Which of the two names should be canonical if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\\\",\\n  \\\"alias\\\": \\\"The other name if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\\\",\\n  \\\"reason\\\": \\\"brief justification\\\"\\n}}\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\nALIAS_RESOLUTION_SYSTEM = \\\"\\\"\\\"You are a literary analyst identifying character aliases.\\n\\nYour task is to determine which character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: Titles, ranks, and honorifics should be IGNORED when matching names.\\nThe underlying PERSON is what matters, not how they're addressed. Examples:\\n- Military: \\\"SSgt Otto\\\" = \\\"Staff Sergeant Mark Otto\\\" = \\\"Mark Otto\\\" (but NOT just \\\"Otto\\\" alone)\\n- Civilian: \\\"Mr. Smith\\\" = \\\"John Smith\\\" (but NOT just \\\"Smith\\\" alone - could be family member)\\n- Religious: \\\"Father O'Brien\\\" = \\\"Patrick O'Brien\\\" (but NOT just \\\"O'Brien\\\" alone)\\n- Academic: \\\"Professor Williams\\\" = \\\"Dr. Williams\\\" = \\\"Jane Williams\\\"\\n\\nCRITICAL RULES - BE CONSERVATIVE:\\n1. Only merge names if they CLEARLY refer to the same individual with HIGH CONFIDENCE\\n2. Different people with similar names should NEVER be merged\\n3. Pay close attention to chapter appearances and context clues\\n4. When in doubt, keep names SEPARATE - false negatives are better than false positives\\n5. A bare FIRST name can merge with a full name (e.g., \\\"Mike\\\" → \\\"Michael Anderson\\\")\\n6. A bare LAST name is RISKY - only merge if you're absolutely certain (could be family member)\\n\\nFor the canonical name, prefer the FULL CIVILIAN NAME (first + last) over titled versions.\\n\\nNEVER merge:\\n- Different characters who happen to share only a first name (e.g., two characters named Michael)\\n- Different characters who share ONLY a last name (family members, spouses, siblings)\\n  - \\\"Mr. Anderson\\\" (father) ≠ \\\"Michael\\\" (son) even if both are Andersons\\n  - \\\"Robert Wilson\\\" ≠ \\\"Mrs. Wilson\\\" (his wife Emma)\\n- Characters who appear in completely different parts of the book with no overlap\\n- Names that are clearly different major characters (check mention counts - if both have 50+ mentions, they're probably separate people!)\\n\\nBE EXTREMELY CAUTIOUS WITH:\\n- Bare last names (could be family members)\\n- Gendered titles (Mr. vs Mrs. vs Miss suggests different people)\\n- Characters with similar but different names (e.g., \\\"Michael\\\" vs \\\"Michelle\\\")\\n\\nSPECIFIC ANTI-PATTERNS (DO NOT MERGE):\\n- Different first names + same last name (likely family members): spouses, siblings, parents/children\\n- Single-word names with no overlap: completely different characters with unrelated names\\n- Characters who co-occur but have different identities: appearing together ≠ being the same person\\n\\nONLY merge when confident:\\n1. Full name + partial name: full name contains the partial name as a component\\n2. Title variations: titled form and untitled form of the same name\\n3. Obvious nickname patterns: common nickname/full name pairs\\n\\nCRITICAL MERGE PATTERNS:\\n\\nMERGE these patterns (HIGH CONFIDENCE):\\n- Full name variations: \\\"FirstName LastName\\\" = \\\"LastName\\\" = \\\"Title LastName\\\"\\n- Nickname patterns: Shortened forms or diminutives of the same character\\n- Birth/married names: Known alternate names for the same person (when contextually clear)\\n\\nDO NOT MERGE these patterns:\\n- Title-only matches: Characters sharing only a title but different last names are DIFFERENT people\\n- Family members: Different first names + same last name typically indicates spouses, siblings, or family (separate characters)\\n- Similar titles: Same professional title (Professor, Doctor, Captain) doesn't indicate same person\\n\\nCONTEXTUAL CLUES to consider:\\n- Relationship descriptors: \\\"his/her spouse\\\", \\\"his/her parent\\\", \\\"his/her sibling\\\" indicate SEPARATE characters\\n- Gendered pronouns: Consistent he/she usage helps distinguish characters with ambiguous names\\n- First-person narrative: First-person \\\"I\\\" may indicate narrator (check for self-references)\\n\\nMENTION COUNT HEURISTIC:\\n- Large disparity (e.g., 200+ vs <10 mentions) often indicates full name vs shortened form\\n- Similar counts (both >20 mentions) often indicates distinct characters of comparable importance\\n\\nWhen in doubt, keep SEPARATE. False negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different characters).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\nALIAS_RESOLUTION_PROMPT = \\\"\\\"\\\"Identify which character names refer to the SAME PERSON in this novel.\\n\",\"numLines\":150,\"startLine\":95,\"totalLines\":2176}}}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_017ATmE3DkskNqd4yHsnMd8z\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"  2049→    def _llm_cross_group_resolution(\\n  2050→        self,\\n  2051→        epithet_groups: dict[str, list[CharacterValidationResult]],\\n  2052→        proper_name_groups: dict[str, list[CharacterValidationResult]],\\n  2053→    ) -> dict[str, str]:\\n  2054→        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n  2055→        Use LLM to link epithets to proper names (e.g., \\\"the prince\\\" -> \\\"Prince Prospero\\\").\\n  2056→\\n  2057→        Returns:\\n  2058→            dict mapping epithet_name -> proper_name for confirmed matches\\n  2059→        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n  2060→        if not self.llm or len(epithet_groups) == 0 or len(proper_name_groups) == 0:\\n  2061→            return {}\\n  2062→\\n  2063→        # Build prompt with epithet info\\n  2064→        epithet_lines = []\\n  2065→        sorted_epithets = sorted(\\n  2066→            epithet_groups.items(),\\n  2067→            key=lambda x: -sum(r.proposal.mention_count for r in x[1]),\\n  2068→        )[:15]  # Limit to top 15 epithets to keep prompt manageable\\n  2069→\\n  2070→        for name, results in sorted_epithets:\\n  2071→            total_mentions = sum(r.proposal.mention_count for r in results)\\n  2072→            chapters_str = self._chapters_for_name(name, epithet_groups, max_chapters=8)\\n  2073→            context_str = self._format_contexts(results, max_contexts=4, max_chars=150)\\n  2074→            epithet_lines.append(f\\\"- {name} ({total_mentions} mentions, chapters: {chapters_str})\\\")\\n  2075→            epithet_lines.append(f\\\"  Context: {context_str}\\\")\\n  2076→\\n  2077→        # Build prompt with proper name info\\n  2078→        proper_name_lines = []\\n  2079→        sorted_proper = sorted(\\n  2080→            proper_name_groups.items(),\\n  2081→            key=lambda x: -sum(r.proposal.mention_count for r in x[1]),\\n  2082→        )[:15]  # Limit to top 15 proper names\\n  2083→\\n  2084→        for name, results in sorted_proper:\\n  2085→            total_mentions = sum(r.proposal.mention_count for r in results)\\n  2086→            chapters_str = self._chapters_for_name(name, proper_name_groups, max_chapters=8)\\n  2087→            context_str = self._format_contexts(results, max_contexts=3, max_chars=120)\\n  2088→            proper_name_lines.append(f\\\"- {name} ({total_mentions} mentions, chapters: {chapters_str})\\\")\\n  2089→            proper_name_lines.append(f\\\"  Context: {context_str}\\\")\\n  2090→\\n  2091→        epithets_str = \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(epithet_lines)\\n  2092→        proper_names_str = \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(proper_name_lines)\\n  2093→        prompt = CROSS_GROUP_PROMPT.format(epithets=epithets_str, proper_names=proper_names_str)\\n  2094→\\n  2095→        try:\\n  2096→            result, response = self.llm.query_json(prompt, system=CROSS_GROUP_SYSTEM)\\n  2097→\\n  2098→            if result is None or not isinstance(result, list):\\n  2099→                logger.warning(\\\"LLM cross-group resolution returned invalid response\\\")\\n  2100→                return {}\\n  2101→\\n  2102→            # Parse response into epithet -> proper_name mapping\\n  2103→            cross_map = {}\\n  2104→            for match in result:\\n  2105→                if not isinstance(match, dict):\\n  2106→                    continue\\n  2107→\\n  2108→                epithet = match.get(\\\"epithet\\\", \\\"\\\")\\n  2109→                proper_name = match.get(\\\"proper_name\\\", \\\"\\\")\\n  2110→\\n  2111→                if not epithet or not proper_name:\\n  2112→                    continue\\n  2113→\\n  2114→                # Match to actual names (fuzzy matching to handle LLM variations)\\n  2115→                matched_epithet = self._find_closest_name(epithet, epithet_groups.keys())\\n  2116→                matched_proper = self._find_closest_name(proper_name, proper_name_groups.keys())\\n  2117→\\n  2118→                if matched_epithet and matched_proper:\\n  2119→                    # Pre-filter: Check if they appear in confrontational relationship\\n  2120→                    epithet_results = epithet_groups.get(matched_epithet, [])\\n  2121→                    proper_results = proper_name_groups.get(matched_proper, [])\\n  2122→\\n  2123→                    if self._entities_in_confrontation(matched_epithet, matched_proper, epithet_results, proper_results):\\n  2124→                        logger.info(f\\\"Skipping cross-group match due to confrontation: '{matched_epithet}' vs '{matched_proper}'\\\")\\n  2125→                        continue\\n  2126→\\n  2127→                    cross_map[matched_epithet] = matched_proper\\n  2128→                    logger.info(f\\\"Cross-group match: epithet '{matched_epithet}' -> proper name '{matched_proper}'\\\")\\n  2129→\\n  2130→            return cross_map\\n  2131→\\n  2132→        except Exception as e:\\n  2133→            logger.error(f\\\"LLM cross-group resolution failed: {e}\\\")\\n  2134→            return {}\\n  2135→\\n  2136→    def _pick_canonical_epithet(\\n  2137→        self,\\n  2138→        epithets: list[str],\\n  2139→        epithet_groups: dict[str, list[CharacterValidationResult]],\\n  2140→    ) -> str:\\n  2141→        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n  2142→        Pick the best canonical name from a group of epithet aliases.\\n  2143→\\n  2144→        Priority:\\n  2145→        1. Specificity: more words = more specific (e.g., \\\"the blind old man\\\" > \\\"the old man\\\")\\n  2146→        2. Uniqueness: less common phrases are more unique\\n  2147→        3. Frequency: higher mention count indicates more established usage\\n  2148→        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n  2149→        if len(epithets) == 1:\\n  2150→            return epithets[0]\\n  2151→\\n  2152→        def score(epithet: str) -> tuple[int, int, int]:\\n  2153→            # Word count (more = more specific)\\n  2154→            words = epithet.lower().split()\\n  2155→            word_count = len(words)\\n  2156→\\n  2157→            # Uniqueness: inverse of how common each word is\\n  2158→            # More unique words = better canonical\\n  2159→            common_words = {'the', 'a', 'an', 'old', 'young', 'man', 'woman', 'person'}\\n  2160→            unique_words = sum(1 for w in words if w not in common_words)\\n  2161→\\n  2162→            # Mention count\\n  2163→            results = epithet_groups.get(epithet, [])\\n  2164→            mention_count = sum(r.proposal.mention_count for r in results)\\n  2165→\\n  2166→            return (word_count, unique_words, mention_count)\\n  2167→\\n  2168→        return max(epithets, key=score)\\n  2169→\\n  2170→    def _generate_id(self, name: str) -> str:\\n  2171→        \\\"\\\"\\\"Generate a unique ID for a character.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n  2172→        # Use hash of lowercase name for consistency\\n  2173→        name_hash = hashlib.md5(name.lower().encode()).hexdigest()[:8]\\n  2174→        safe_name = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z0-9]', '_', name.lower())[:20]\\n  2175→        return f\\\"char_{safe_name}_{name_hash}\\\"\\n  2176→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"10a8e652-7846-4dba-89c8-03dc3ba0817f\",\"uuid\":\"9b72311d-d5b6-4ba5-9f17-0094b01482a5\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py\",\"content\":\"    def _llm_cross_group_resolution(\\n        self,\\n        epithet_groups: dict[str, list[CharacterValidationResult]],\\n        proper_name_groups: dict[str, list[CharacterValidationResult]],\\n    ) -> dict[str, str]:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        Use LLM to link epithets to proper names (e.g., \\\"the prince\\\" -> \\\"Prince Prospero\\\").\\n\\n        Returns:\\n            dict mapping epithet_name -> proper_name for confirmed matches\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        if not self.llm or len(epithet_groups) == 0 or len(proper_name_groups) == 0:\\n            return {}\\n\\n        # Build prompt with epithet info\\n        epithet_lines = []\\n        sorted_epithets = sorted(\\n            epithet_groups.items(),\\n            key=lambda x: -sum(r.proposal.mention_count for r in x[1]),\\n        )[:15]  # Limit to top 15 epithets to keep prompt